WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier
Reviewer: 

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.415
For decades now,

00:00:14.415 --> 00:00:17.209
we’ve been saying we should
reduce our emissions,

00:00:17.209 --> 00:00:18.794
but they’ve kept increasing.

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:23.841
One of the key reasons
is we don’t measure accurately

00:00:23.841 --> 00:00:26.427
the climate impact of our actions.

00:00:27.011 --> 00:00:29.722
Imagine trying to save money,

00:00:29.722 --> 00:00:31.057
but when you go shopping,

00:00:31.057 --> 00:00:34.810
there is no price tag on any item ...

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.063
or trying to lose weight,

00:00:37.063 --> 00:00:40.316
but you cannot measure
the portion sizes and the calories.

00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.109
You would be bound to fail.

00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:47.323
This level of blindness
is close to the one we have

00:00:47.323 --> 00:00:49.200
when it comes to our climate impact.

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:52.828
Measuring greenhouse gas
emissions is hard.

00:00:53.704 --> 00:00:55.122
It has no color,

00:00:55.122 --> 00:00:56.957
it has no smell;

00:00:56.957 --> 00:00:58.375
it’s invisible.

00:00:58.751 --> 00:01:01.962
We cannot put sensors everywhere,

00:01:01.962 --> 00:01:03.964
on every building,

00:01:03.964 --> 00:01:05.424
every track,

00:01:05.424 --> 00:01:06.759
every field,

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:08.886
every cow --

00:01:08.886 --> 00:01:10.179
so most of the time,

00:01:10.179 --> 00:01:12.765
we give up and we don’t measure.

00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:15.267
And when we do measure,

00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:18.479
we are reduced to relying on estimations

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:19.980
and conversion factors.

00:01:21.148 --> 00:01:26.445
The consequence is we end up working
with highly incomplete

00:01:26.445 --> 00:01:29.198
and inaccurate estimations
of our emissions.

00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:33.994
Often we have a margin of error
of 30 to 60 percent.

00:01:34.703 --> 00:01:40.167
This means targets
and action plans are set

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.253
based on inaccurate data.

00:01:43.087 --> 00:01:44.797
If we look at the corporations

00:01:44.797 --> 00:01:49.093
that report their progress
on climate to the CDP,

00:01:49.093 --> 00:01:53.722
which is a nonprofit organization
that runs a global disclosure system

00:01:53.722 --> 00:01:56.725
for environmental impacts,

00:01:56.725 --> 00:01:59.478
what we see is striking:

00:01:59.478 --> 00:02:02.773
more than two-thirds of the companies

00:02:02.773 --> 00:02:06.402
are not accurately measuring
their emissions,

00:02:06.402 --> 00:02:10.447
and only seven percent of those companies

00:02:10.447 --> 00:02:14.535
are ultimately reducing
their impact in some way.

00:02:15.661 --> 00:02:19.582
You cannot reduce
what you cannot measure.

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:26.589
It is key for corporations to be able
to measure across all activities,

00:02:26.589 --> 00:02:30.509
all sources that drive carbon up or down.

00:02:31.218 --> 00:02:32.261
In a way,

00:02:32.261 --> 00:02:36.015
that’s just putting the same rigor
to carbon measurements

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:39.226
that we have for financial accounting.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:46.025
It took more than 100 years to put modern,
automated financial accounting in place.

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:50.446
We don’t have 100 years
when it comes to climate.

00:02:51.238 --> 00:02:57.244
But this is crucial for corporations
to set meaningful targets

00:02:57.244 --> 00:02:59.079
and successful action plans.

00:02:59.997 --> 00:03:03.167
One of the most powerful tools we have

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:07.630
to help us accelerate on this journey 
is artificial intelligence.

00:03:08.297 --> 00:03:12.927
Artificial intelligence can process
data automatically

00:03:12.927 --> 00:03:15.971
from diverse, unstructured sources

00:03:15.971 --> 00:03:18.849
like invoices, consumer behavior data.

00:03:20.059 --> 00:03:25.439
It can work by modeling to better estimate
the missing information.

00:03:26.815 --> 00:03:30.569
It can simulate and ultimately
optimize emissions.

00:03:31.487 --> 00:03:33.864
Let me share an example
of how this could work.

00:03:34.907 --> 00:03:37.785
A wine and spirits international company:

00:03:37.785 --> 00:03:39.453
billions of sales,

00:03:39.453 --> 00:03:40.621
hundreds of brands,

00:03:40.621 --> 00:03:42.331
consumers across the globe.

00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:45.542
When they want to measure their impact,

00:03:45.542 --> 00:03:49.421
they need to measure
across the entire set of their emissions.

00:03:50.381 --> 00:03:53.592
This means direct emissions
from facilities,

00:03:53.592 --> 00:03:55.511
purchased electricity,

00:03:55.511 --> 00:03:56.971
raw materials,

00:03:56.971 --> 00:03:58.097
leased assets,

00:03:58.097 --> 00:03:59.598
IT emissions

00:03:59.598 --> 00:04:00.683
business travel,

00:04:00.683 --> 00:04:02.017
transportation,

00:04:02.017 --> 00:04:03.102
waste,

00:04:03.102 --> 00:04:04.436
product end of life,

00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:05.813
etcetera, etcetera.

00:04:06.397 --> 00:04:09.316
That’s a huge amount
of information to collect.

00:04:10.025 --> 00:04:13.988
And most of it is actually inaccessible
to the company itself

00:04:13.988 --> 00:04:17.366
because it comes from outside
its direct scope of activity.

00:04:17.908 --> 00:04:18.909
For example,

00:04:18.909 --> 00:04:23.205
from suppliers that are not yet able
to calculate their emissions either.

00:04:24.248 --> 00:04:29.461
So when the sustainability team
calculates their impact,

00:04:29.461 --> 00:04:32.798
they have no choice
but to do rough estimates.

00:04:33.507 --> 00:04:35.592
Let’s examine the glass for bottles.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.555
The way they calculate
glass emissions is the following.

00:04:40.514 --> 00:04:44.226
They take the total amount
of glass bought last year --

00:04:44.226 --> 00:04:45.811
let’s say 1,000 tons.

00:04:46.687 --> 00:04:50.274
They multiply it by a conversion factor,

00:04:50.274 --> 00:04:56.155
which represents the average kilos
of CO2 equivalent for one ton of glass --

00:04:56.155 --> 00:04:57.906
let’s say 950.

00:04:58.365 --> 00:05:02.536
950 x 1000 makes 950,000.

00:05:02.995 --> 00:05:05.164
Of course this is hugely inaccurate

00:05:05.164 --> 00:05:07.249
because it does not take into account

00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:12.254
all the numerous factors
that impact actual emissions,

00:05:12.254 --> 00:05:16.383
so it’s hard to set targets
and action plans.

00:05:16.884 --> 00:05:21.096
This is where the sustainability team
calls data scientists

00:05:21.096 --> 00:05:27.061
to come in and process detailed data
about the type of glass,

00:05:27.061 --> 00:05:28.771
the color of the glass,

00:05:28.771 --> 00:05:30.189
the recycling share,

00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:32.441
the supplier country of origin,

00:05:32.441 --> 00:05:33.984
the transportation mode,

00:05:33.984 --> 00:05:34.985
by brand,

00:05:34.985 --> 00:05:35.986
by product.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:40.240
They can simulate the design
and the supply chain

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.117
and integrate in the calculation

00:05:42.117 --> 00:05:44.453
the importance of the glass color --

00:05:44.453 --> 00:05:47.956
1.5 times more emissions
for a clear bottle

00:05:47.956 --> 00:05:50.000
versus a green bottle;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.920
the importance of the country of origin --

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:57.257
twice the amount of emissions
for one country versus another one,

00:05:57.257 --> 00:05:59.385
depending on the energy mix;

00:05:59.385 --> 00:06:02.137
the importance of the design itself --

00:06:02.137 --> 00:06:04.056
for the same total weight,

00:06:04.056 --> 00:06:09.311
1.5 times more emissions
for one design versus another one.

00:06:09.937 --> 00:06:13.732
Instead of having one big, average number,

00:06:13.732 --> 00:06:18.404
you now have a model
which correlates and calculates emissions

00:06:18.404 --> 00:06:19.822
at a granular level.

00:06:20.989 --> 00:06:22.908
With this type of methodology,

00:06:22.908 --> 00:06:27.830
the emissions figure is typically
corrected by 30 to 50 percent.

00:06:28.747 --> 00:06:30.249
And more importantly,

00:06:30.249 --> 00:06:33.585
the company can now move to action

00:06:33.585 --> 00:06:37.256
as they can, one, set meaningful targets,

00:06:37.256 --> 00:06:40.634
two, identify very concrete initiatives,

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:41.635
and three,

00:06:41.635 --> 00:06:44.888
recalculate emissions over time
and measure their progress.

00:06:46.098 --> 00:06:48.517
Let me share another example:

00:06:48.517 --> 00:06:49.518
cement.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.813
Cement is a massive CO2 emitter.

00:06:53.981 --> 00:06:56.108
If cement were a country,

00:06:56.108 --> 00:06:59.486
it would rank as
the third-largest emitter,

00:06:59.486 --> 00:07:01.947
right after China and the US,

00:07:01.947 --> 00:07:04.658
in front of the European Union and India.

00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:10.414
Most of the emissions come from
the process of producing clinker,

00:07:10.414 --> 00:07:12.332
the key ingredient in cement.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.376
To produce clinker,

00:07:14.376 --> 00:07:19.131
you need to maintain a temperature
of over 1,400 degrees Celsius.

00:07:19.715 --> 00:07:22.050
It requires a lot of fuel,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.970
and it’s really just carbon
containing the whole materials.

00:07:25.637 --> 00:07:31.101
So the secret sauce is to produce
cleaner and higher quality clinker,

00:07:31.101 --> 00:07:33.395
because the higher
the quality of the clinker,

00:07:33.395 --> 00:07:37.149
the less of it you will need
to produce cement ultimately,

00:07:37.149 --> 00:07:39.735
and therefore the less emissions
you will generate.

00:07:40.652 --> 00:07:44.823
But producing high-quality clinker
is a complex science.

00:07:45.365 --> 00:07:49.161
It depends on multiple factors
that influence each other.

00:07:49.411 --> 00:07:51.914
For example, the process parameters,

00:07:51.914 --> 00:07:54.416
like the rotation speed of the machine,

00:07:54.416 --> 00:07:56.335
how quickly you fill it,

00:07:56.335 --> 00:07:58.253
the type of fuel you use,

00:07:58.253 --> 00:08:01.632
the raw materials
and their exact chemical composition.

00:08:02.674 --> 00:08:07.554
This is where artificial intelligence
can again have an enormous impact.

00:08:07.846 --> 00:08:12.267
On-site operational teams
are trying to manually maintain

00:08:12.267 --> 00:08:14.645
the best set of parameters possible.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:20.400
AI can help by measuring better
through different sources,

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.985
like direct measurements,

00:08:21.985 --> 00:08:23.237
material and mass balance,

00:08:23.237 --> 00:08:24.321
etcetera ...

00:08:24.321 --> 00:08:27.533
simulate all the potential decisions

00:08:27.533 --> 00:08:30.994
and recommend the optimal ones
to the operators.

00:08:31.662 --> 00:08:35.707
These techniques implemented
in a cement production process

00:08:35.707 --> 00:08:39.419
enable a substantial emissions reduction

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:40.796
in a matter of months.

00:08:42.172 --> 00:08:45.300
There is an infinity
of applications possible.

00:08:46.009 --> 00:08:47.886
There is no company,

00:08:47.886 --> 00:08:52.766
no industry that cannot derive
significant climate impact

00:08:52.766 --> 00:08:55.018
from the use of artificial intelligence.

00:08:55.644 --> 00:08:59.648
I’m not saying artificial intelligence
alone will save us.

00:09:00.607 --> 00:09:02.943
But artificial intelligence,

00:09:02.943 --> 00:09:06.029
by helping us measure accurately,

00:09:06.029 --> 00:09:07.197
simulate

00:09:07.197 --> 00:09:09.241
and optimize,

00:09:09.241 --> 00:09:13.036
enables significant emissions reduction

00:09:13.036 --> 00:09:16.707
in a quite fast, cheap and easy way.

00:09:17.583 --> 00:09:20.752
We cannot miss this opportunity.

00:09:22.212 --> 00:09:23.213
Thank you.

